{
  "teamId": 119,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Los Angeles Dodgers opened their championship campaign with immediate fireworks, setting the tone for a season of offensive dominance and star power. On Opening Day, Teoscar Hern\u00e1ndez launched a three-run blast to center field, plating Shohei Ohtani and Mookie Betts in a moment that announced the Dodgers' intentions to the rest of baseball. The very next night, Will Smith's clutch single rescued a tense situation, and early April brought a pair of massive blows from Max Muncy and Ohtani himself. Los Angeles was not a team that intended to wait around.\n\nThe middle months told a story of excellence punctuated by frustrating cracks. Tanner Scott, tasked with closing games, became both a hero and a source of anxiety \u2014 his name appearing repeatedly on the wrong side of the ledger as opponents found ways to punish him in high-leverage moments. A brutal September 6th outing, in which a single he allowed led to Gunnar Henderson and Jorge Mateo scoring in a -0.724 WPA collapse, stood as the low point of the bullpen's struggles. Yet the offense kept answering every setback. Shohei Ohtani's 52nd home run of the season in late September, a three-run shot that scored Andy Pages and Enrique Hern\u00e1ndez, was a reminder that the Dodgers possessed one of the most dangerous lineups in baseball history.\n\nFrequent clutch contributions came from unexpected corners. Freddie Freeman's line drive single on July 23rd, scoring Betts and Ohtani and registering a staggering 0.753 WPA, was the single biggest regular-season swing of the year. Max Muncy's grand slam in June, Andy Pages' 25th home run in September, and a parade of Will Smith clutch moments stitched together a 93-win season that was never truly in doubt even when the bullpen wobbled. The Dodgers scored 825 runs, clubbed 244 home runs, and carried a relentless sense of inevitability into October.\n\nThe postseason confirmed what the regular season had suggested: this team was built for October. Teoscar Hern\u00e1ndez opened the Division Series with a three-run home run, and a fortunate fielding error by pitcher Orion Kerkering kept a rally alive in the decisive moments of the NLDS. Blake Treinen slammed the door in the Championship Series with a key strikeout of Brice Turang. The World Series against the Toronto Blue Jays brought the grandest moments of all \u2014 Freddie Freeman homered, Miguel Rojas added a surprise blast, Will Smith put the exclamation mark on Game 7 with a go-ahead home run, and Yoshinobu Yamamoto induced a game-sealing double play that sent Dodger Blue into delirium. Los Angeles stood alone as World Series champions once more.",
